extraction_notes: "Printed 117-118; experience ladder ''Snakebird & Mystic'', printed 233, stored as the lower bound of each band. THE SPELLS ARE NAMED LISTS drawn from the book''s own index of invocations (printed 126-127), one name per spell the chapter describes: 131. Summon Entity is left off - the index lists it at level 12 and the chapter prints no stat block for it, so there is no row (zzzzzzzzzzzzz-nb-spells.sql). Every name is the row a Nightbane character sees, checked against production 2026-09-16: 27 plain Nightbane invocations; 12 `Nightbane: <name>` retellings, ten whose printed cost differs from the shared row and two (Wards, Summon and Control Rain) whose only other row is Rifts''; and 92 shared rows under the catalog''s spelling, which differs from the index for Fingers of the Wind, Negate Poison/Toxin, Swim as a Fish (lesser) and (Superior), Invisibility (Superior), Purification, Spoil, Summon and Control Canines, Control & Enslave Entity and Summon and Control Rodents. The levels are the book''s and match every row. The fourth-level-and-up rule, two spells from any level up to the mystic''s own, is each entry''s list PLUS `spell_levels: up_to_character_level` (BOOK-INGEST-AUDIT F61). ''Nor is he likely to read symbols, runes or to perform rituals'' is a tendency, not a bar: the Ritual invocations stay on the lists and the sentence is on the Intuitive Magic ability. THE PSIONICS ARE NAMED LISTS, not a category gate, read off this book''s category lists on printed 69. The Psychic Sensitive list holds five powers the catalog files elsewhere (Death Trance, Mind Block, Summon Inner Strength under Physical; Empathic Transmission, Hypnotic Suggestion under Super), and the Healer list two (Impervious to Cold and to Fire, Physical). Book names resolved: Suggestion is Hypnotic Suggestion (survey D7), Object Read/Psychometry is Object Read (Psychometry), Increase Healing is Increased Healing. The Healer list''s Healing is described nowhere and has no row. The four granted powers are left off the pick lists. ''+1 to save vs magic'' is unqualified and is stored as both spell_magic and ritual_magic. Spell strength has no sheet field: it is the Spell Strength ability and level_progression. Money: the average mystic''s 2D6x100 cash is stored; the 1D6x1,000 in possessions is not cash and is not stored, and the wealth-oriented alternative (4D4x1,000 cash, 4D6x10,000 possessions) is in GM Notes. Equipment: the page names walking shoes, boots, gloves and a hat but no model, so each is a choice among the chapter''s own rows; the rain/trench coat is Raincoat. Travelling clothes, a mirror, a portable radio, a CD player and a walking stick are sold nowhere in the equipment chapter and are stubs. Personal items are not stored. Skills: Read/Speak Native Language 98% is stored as Language: Native Tongue and Literacy: Native Language, both 98. This book files Wilderness Survival under Espionage (printed 48) and grants it there at +10%; the catalog files it under Wilderness, which the page also grants at +5%; the book''s Espionage line, and its +10%, is what the class states for that skill. Hand to hand is not granted: it costs one, two or three related selections, and is in the related-skill note. The page states no hit points and no S.D.C."
---

## Lore

Sorcerers with their books and scrolls and complex studies represent one side
of the magical coin. The other is found among primitive peoples - rainmakers,
shamans and medicine men. A mystic''s magical knowledge is intuitive rather than
rational: he simply accepts that he suddenly knows something, and has learned to
trust his feelings and hunches. Where sorcerers research and practice, mystics
meditate and set formal education aside to follow a cosmic or spiritual path.
They open themselves to the realms of magic, and their magic is no less
effective for being granted from outside themselves.

Their connection to the natural psychic emanations of the world gives mystics
psionic powers as well, above all the power to sense the supernatural. Mystics
in a hundred cultures knew the world was being invaded moments before Dark Day
began, and many have chosen to fight the enemy at all costs.

Most live in cultures removed from the chaos and materialism of the "civilized"
world. In the U.S. many are Native Americans living on reservations, Caribbean
practitioners of Voudoun or Santeria, gypsies, neo-pagans, Taoist mystics and
the like. The ways of mysticism can be taught - philosophy, meditation, and an
opening of mind and body to the world - but many mystics claim nobody learns to
become one: a mystic is born with the gift.

Most mystics own very little: walking shoes, boots, gloves, a hat, travelling
clothes, a rain or trench coat, a knapsack and backpack, a mirror, a portable
radio or CD player, a staff or walking stick, and personal items.

## GM Notes

**The wealth-oriented mystic.** A few mystics who pursue power, wealth and
prestige end up owning a huge mansion, a fleet of vehicles and other symbols of
wealth. They are very rare now, because they were easy to find and were
destroyed in the days following Dark Day. Such a mystic starts with 4D4x1,000
dollars in cash and 4D6x10,000 in possessions (housing, vehicles and equipment)
instead of the average mystic''s 2D6x100 in cash and 1D6x1,000 in possessions.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'nb-mystic');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'nb-mystic';
SELECT count(*) AS stub_gear FROM gear WHERE slug IN ('travelling-clothes-nb', 'mirror-nb', 'portable-radio-nb', 'portable-cd-player-nb', 'walking-stick-nb');

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-nb-mystic-class.sql');
